WEBVTT

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:01.974
A trigger is a rule stored in the

00:00:01.987 --> 00:00:02.737
database.

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:05.776
It watches for a change to a table and

00:00:05.776 --> 00:00:07.737
then reacts automatically.

00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:11.158
Let's see how SQL:1999 describes one.

00:00:11.986 --> 00:00:13.960
Every trigger has three parts.

00:00:14.394 --> 00:00:16.302
The event says which change to watch:

00:00:16.736 --> 00:00:19.420
an insert, a delete or an update on a

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:19.881
table.

00:00:20.302 --> 00:00:22.710
The condition is an optional when clause.

00:00:23.144 --> 00:00:25.736
The action is an SQL procedural statement

00:00:25.947 --> 00:00:28.486
that runs if the condition holds.

00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:31.449
You also choose when the trigger runs.

00:00:31.844 --> 00:00:34.291
A before trigger runs ahead of the change,

00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:36.909
so it can check a modification and shape

00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:37.580
its effect.

00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:39.988
It is not allowed to update the database

00:00:39.988 --> 00:00:40.567
directly.

00:00:40.988 --> 00:00:42.975
It can only adjust the values of the row

00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:45.186
being written, with a statement like set

00:00:45.186 --> 00:00:47.344
new dot t equals an expression.

00:00:48.169 --> 00:00:50.551
An after trigger runs once the change has

00:00:50.551 --> 00:00:51.103
been made.

00:00:51.524 --> 00:00:53.932
This is the most common mode and suits

00:00:53.945 --> 00:00:55.143
most applications.

00:00:55.616 --> 00:00:57.722
Follow the flow from the statement to the

00:00:57.735 --> 00:00:58.643
final trigger.

00:00:59.452 --> 00:01:00.807
Next is granularity.

00:01:01.242 --> 00:01:03.610
With row-level granularity, the trigger is

00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:05.926
considered once for every row the

00:01:05.926 --> 00:01:07.031
statement touches.

00:01:07.478 --> 00:01:09.610
Think of a guard checking each person who

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:10.728
walks through a door.

00:01:11.149 --> 00:01:13.242
It is simpler to write, but it can be less

00:01:13.242 --> 00:01:13.913
efficient.

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:16.807
With statement-level granularity, the

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.241
trigger is considered once for the whole

00:01:19.373 --> 00:01:22.386
activating statement, however many rows

00:01:22.465 --> 00:01:23.346
are affected.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.583
This is closer to how SQL normally works,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.767
on whole sets of rows at a time.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.747
Triggers can look at the data before and

00:01:31.879 --> 00:01:33.550
after the change through transition

00:01:33.563 --> 00:01:34.300
variables.

00:01:34.708 --> 00:01:37.392
In a row-level trigger, old and new are

00:01:37.405 --> 00:01:39.852
tuple variables holding one row's values

00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:40.931
before and after.

00:01:41.352 --> 00:01:43.852
In a statement-level trigger, old table

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:47.287
and new table hold all the affected rows.

00:01:48.131 --> 00:01:50.579
Some variables don't exist for every

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:51.026
event.

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:53.408
An insert has no earlier version of the

00:01:53.408 --> 00:01:56.079
row, and a delete has no later version.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.237
So think about which event your trigger

00:01:58.237 --> 00:02:00.684
watches before you use these variables.
